{"chunk_id": "0bdc14d02de4", "text": "This section transitions from Vidura\u2019s counsel to the philosophical teaching of the sage Sanat\u2011sujata. Dhritarashtra, troubled and curious, asks whether death truly exists. Sanat\u2011sujata replies with a metaphysical argument: what humans call death is not merely physical destruction but ignorance. Ignorance binds beings to desire, anger, pride, and attachment, which in turn keep them trapped in cycles of rebirth. Knowledge of Brahman\u2014the true nature of the self\u2014brings immortality. The dialogue also contrasts paths of ritual action, virtue, and renunciation, suggesting that while righteous deeds and sacrifices have value, true liberation comes through knowledge and mastery of desire. The passage matters because it introduces a major philosophical theme in the Mahabharata: inner ignorance, not physical death, is the real enemy, and self\u2011knowledge is the path to freedom.\nVidura summons sage Sanat-sujata, who teaches Dhritarashtra that ignorance and desire are true death while knowledge and self-restraint bring immortality.\nThemes: ignorance as death, self-knowledge and immortality, desire and downfall, action vs renunciation, spiritual instruction to a king", "vector": [0.024886131286621094, 0.007150658871978521, 0.0007214680081233382, -0.013618426397442818, -0.0009718067012727261, 0.01843806356191635, -0.04128865525126457, -0.015207462012767792, -0.02770962379872799, -0.0006402105209417641, 0.023192036896944046, 0.013375475071370602, 0.0066023762337863445, 0.01943613402545452, -0.0035490645095705986, -0.00823409203439951, 0.0498247928917408, -0.00551565969362855, 0.03939757123589516, -0.022587940096855164, -0.008509875275194645, -0.008516441099345684, 0.004780238494277, 0.017663244158029556, -0.0007707149488851428, 0.020709989592432976, 0.01413059514015913, -0.037611547857522964, -0.005220178049057722, -0.017426857724785805, -0.0014938245294615626, -0.011858668178319931, -0.01682276278734207, -0.02973